detail.abstract:
I study whether U.S. industries that were more exposed, ex ante, to large-language-model-era artificial intelligence exhibit differential changes after 2022 in AI-related discourse and industry outcomes. The analysis uses a balanced NAICS4 industry-year text panel for 2017–2025 and a balanced NAICS4 industry-year structural panel for 2018–2024, merging a predetermined industry AI exposure measure (AIIE) with textual incidence measures of AI discussion and with labor-market and firm-structure out-comes, including employment, wages, payroll, and establishments. My baseline design is a continuous-exposure event-study difference-in-differences specification centered onthe 2022 LLM break. Identification comes from differential within-industry changes over time as a function of pre-period exposure, controlling for NAICS4 fixed effects and common time effects, with stricter sector-by-year fixed effects used as a guardrail against coincident sector-specific shocks. For outcomes with less credible pre-trend behavior under conventional event-study diagnostics, I complement the baseline design with pooled and dynamic synthetic difference-in-differences estimators. Across specifications, more AI-exposed industries exhibit evidence of an initial increase in broad AI-related language, while the more robust post-LLM disclosure divergence is concentrated in strategy- and risk-oriented language. Real-side outcomes point more strongly toward organizational and market reconfiguration than toward broad-based wage gains: establishments and employment-type scale measures rise, while pay-per-worker outcomes are modestly negative in logarithmic specifications. Taken together, the evidence is most consistent with early AI-era divergence in the composition of AI disclosure and in industry organization in more exposed sectors. The paper contributes 
<br>
 new evidence on exposure-based heterogeneity in the early post-LLM period while remaining explicit that the results are strongest as evidence of differential divergence, not as a basis for sweeping welfare or long-run causal claims.&nbsp;

This paper presents the conceptual design, strategic rationale and financial viability assessment of LAEXIM, a proposed asset-light international trading and supply chain start-up based in Vietnam. Operating within the structural inefficiencies of Vietnam's fragmented agricultural sector and underdeveloped export infrastructure, LAEXIM employs a hybrid Import-Export-Supply Chain Services model to connect domestic SME producers with global markets across the Asia-Pacific region, Australasia and the United States.&nbsp;
<div>
 Drawing on primary business planning research, industry data from the Ministry of Industry and Trade of Vietnam (MOIT, 2024) and World Bank commodity market projections (2024), this study contributes to the emerging literature on asset-light internationalization strategies for emerging-market firms. The paper demonstrates that a data-driven, compliance-first trading intermediary can achieve financial break-even within five to eight months of operation, with conservative three-year revenue projections ranging from USD 0.36 million (Year 1) to USD 2.5 million (Year 3), while maintaining a fixed monthly cost base of only USD 1,500-2,500.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The model's competitive advantage rests on four pillars: (1) analytics-driven procurement and price-risk management; (2) a diversified multi-sector portfolio combining high-volume commodities with high-margin premium wine distribution; (3) institutional-grade regulatory compliance infrastructure; and (4) a relationship-centric B2B sales model designed to maximize customer lifetime value. This paper argues that such integrated capabilities are rare among Vietnamese SME traders and constitute a sustainable competitive advantage aligned with the theoretical framework of resource-based competitive advantage (Barney, 1991).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings have implications for practitioners designing new market-entry strategies in frontier agricultural markets, for policymakers seeking to strengthen Vietnamese export capacity and for scholars examining the intersection of digital transformation and international commodity trade in emerging economies.
</div>

Predictive maintenance for commercial bus fleets is critically hindered by severe class imbalance: catastrophic failures such as brake valve seizure or transmission lock-up occur far less frequently than normal operations, leading to high false-negative rates and unexpected roadside breakdowns.&nbsp;
<div>
 We propose a GAN-based rare failure synthesis framework for heterogeneous bus fleets. Unlike conventional oversampling, multiple specialized generators-conditioned on bus model, route topography, and driver patterns-produce realistic time-series sensor data representing rare failure modes. A conditional multi-generator adversarial network synthesizes diverse failure trajectories for each operational context, rebalancing the training distribution without overfitting to sparse real examples.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Validation on a dataset from 450 city buses over 18 months (only 47 rare failures) shows our method improves recall of rare brake system failures from 0.32 to 0.78 while maintaining precision above 0.71, outperforming SMOTE, ADASYN, and single-generator GANs. Fleetwide false alarms decrease by 44%. Additionally, synthesized samples enable smaller depots with no historical rare failures to develop effective predictive models.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results demonstrate that multi-context GAN-based failure synthesis provides a practical pathway toward reliable predictive maintenance for commercial bus fleets, improving fleet availability and reducing catastrophic roadside events.
</div>
